<p><strong>Now that you have the ruler table, how do you use it? The core move is called "cross-validation."</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consensus = strength.</strong> If a company or person ranks high under several different rulers, they're probably genuinely strong. When multiple yardsticks measure the same subject and land on the same answer, that assessment is hard to game.</li>
<li><strong>Divergence = signal.</strong> If a person swings wildly within one list, or varies drastically across lists, that's a signal — often hiding a specific fact: some ruler isn't measuring the right thing.</li>
<li><strong>Blank = blind spot.</strong> What the list doesn't measure still exists. And it may be the most important thing of all. Like "what does this mean for me" — almost no list measures it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hold on to those three rules. Every case study below gets easy with them.</p>
<h2>Three: Deep Dives — Why the Same People Land on Opposite Ends</h2>
<p>### Case One: Yann LeCun — Scholarly Giant, 50th on the Power List</p>
<p>Run all five rulers across Yann LeCun and the picture is instantly clear.</p>
<p>Under the "scholarship & ideas" ruler: father of CNN, one of the deep-learning giants, 2018 Turing Award, longtime research head at Meta. Textbook academic royalty. His rank here should be near-infinite.</p>
<p>Under the "capital power" ruler: 50th on Observer. Why? Because a "power index" isn't measuring scholarly contribution — it's measuring <strong>control over capital and products</strong>. LeCun is a scientist. What he holds is papers and models, not venture funds. He moves AI's thinking, but he doesn't own a product line or dictate capital allocation. This ruler was never built to measure him.</p>
<p>That exposes the DNA of Observer's yardstick: <strong>it measures "whose decisions can change the industry," not "who advanced our understanding."</strong> LeCun at 50 isn't him being weak — it's a ruler that simply doesn't measure the dimension where he's strongest.</p>
<p>The same logic explains why ByteDance's and Alibaba's tech leaders are absent from America's power lists. Not because they lack power — but because the American ruler silently defines "power" as "control over Silicon Valley capital."</p>
<p>### Case Two: OpenAI / Altman — the Consensus Leader Across Three Boards</p>
<p>If one company proves that "consensus = strength," it's OpenAI.</p>
<ul>
<li>On Observer, Altman sits at #1 — capital power maxed out.</li>
<li>On TIME100, it holds a firm place — intellectual influence acknowledged.</li>
<li>On Forbes AI 50, OpenAI tops the list, with funding of $182.6 billion — roughly <strong>60% of all funding across every company on the ranking</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Same subject, three different rulers, one consistent answer. This is the rarest kind of cross-validation: <strong>when multiple yardsticks converge on the same conclusion, that conclusion holds up.</strong></p>
<p>Altman straddling capital, media, and commercial lists says something structural: AI power is concentrating in the hands of a few who simultaneously hold capital, drive the media narrative, and back commercial deployment. That's the core fact of the current landscape — <strong>power is not spreading out; it's tightening.</strong></p>
<p>### Case Three: China's Ruler and America's Ruler</p>
<p>Here's the subtlest part — Chinese AI isn't "absent" from these lists; it's <strong>being measured with a different ruler.</strong></p>
<p>Start with the obvious. Qwen (Alibaba) and Doubao (ByteDance's Volcano Engine) are models ordinary people in China use every day. They do appear on the <strong>Forbes China Top 50</strong> (listed as Qwen and Volcano Engine) — because that ruler measures industry deployment in the Chinese market, and it can see them.</p>
<p>But they're naturally absent from American "power of people" and "private companies" lists. The reason isn't mysterious:</p>
<p><strong>First, their parent companies Alibaba and ByteDance are public.</strong> Forbes AI 50 global explicitly excludes listed companies. Alibaba and ByteDance aren't on it because of a definitional rule — not because of merit.</p>
<p><strong>Second, the American ruler doesn't measure Chinese deployment.</strong> Observer's editors look out from Silicon Valley. They track which capital flows through Silicon Valley and who steers it. The Chinese everyday reality of "putting AI into the lives of hundreds of millions" simply isn't in their viewfinder.</p>
<p>So two companies move in and out of different spreadsheets. <strong>That's not right or wrong — that's two rulers at work.</strong> Miss this and you'll land on one of two equally misleading conclusions: either "Chinese AI is being ignored" or "Chinese AI hasn't earned a seat." One overstates the grievance; the other understates the scale. The truth is in between.</p>
<h2>Four: The Real Landscape — Converging Closings and Unmoved Gaps</h2>
<p>Put the five rulers together and the honest picture is a single, unflashy sentence: <strong>China is closing ground in some dimensions and even leading in places, but on compute, capital, and frontier models, the United States still leads.</strong></p>
<p>China's catching-up — and partially leading — parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Efficiency and cost.</strong> DeepSeek built a globally watched model through pure reinforcement learning and a low-cost training path, landing its paper on the cover of *Nature*. This is a repeatedly documented fact, not marketing.</li>
<li><strong>Open-source ecosystem.</strong> Chinese models are highly open, the communities are active, and the developer ecosystem is compounding.</li>
<li><strong>Deployment scale.</strong> Qwen and Doubao are embedded in the daily life of hundreds of millions of users — a real stockpile on the deployment ruler.</li>
</ul>
<p>And the three where America still leads:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compute.</strong> The supply of high-end chips and the scale of super-compute clusters still sit with the US.</li>
<li><strong>Capital.</strong> A single firm, OpenAI, absorbed six-tenths of the funding across all Forbes AI 50 companies. That density of capital is hard to replicate quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Frontier models.</strong> On the most advanced, most capable general models, the US frontier still leads by half a step to a full length.</li>
</ul>
<p>Then comes the point most easily hijacked by emotion, and it must be said straight:</p>
<p><strong>China's efficiency edge is largely a *forced* result of chip restrictions — not a strategically *chosen* advantage.</strong></p>
<p>With chips cut off, researchers have to do more with less, and that pressure produced a low-cost, high-efficiency path. That's "skill forged under constraint" — it deserves respect, but it isn't proof of "we're winning," and it certainly isn't evidence that "America is done."</p>
<p>The reverse "America is done" story fails just as hard. Compute, capital, and frontier models are still three hard currencies in American hands. Forced efficiency is a skill; it doesn't replace them.</p>
<p><strong>Don't be herded by the two fringe narratives</strong> — one says "Chinese AI has fully surpassed everyone," the other says "Chinese AI can't do anything right." Read a single list and you'll fall into one of them. Read with cross-validation instead.</p>
<h2>Five: What This Means for You — The Lessons for Human-AI Power</h2>
<p>Rankings measure how power is divided. But I want to push the question one level deeper, because it's closer to you than you think.</p>
<p>AI power is flowing toward three kinds of people:</p>
<p><strong>Type one: those who *use* AI.</strong> Their moat isn't knowing a tool — it's <strong>judgment</strong>: knowing which claim to trust, which list is real, which model fits which job. Everyone can use the tool; judgment stays scarce.</p>
<p><strong>Type two: those who *build* AI.</strong> Engineers, researchers, product people. Their moat isn't general technical know-how — it's <strong>understanding of a vertical</strong>: people who truly grasp an industry and a pain point, and can embed a model into it and make it work, are rarer than those who just tune parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Type three: those who *set the rules*.</strong> The people who set standards, allocate capital, and shape institutions. Their moat is <strong>cross-domain connection</strong> — the ones who can translate fluently between tech, business, and policy hold the real leverage.</p>
<p>These three types map onto three different positions — and they point to a deeper observation:</p>
<p><strong>Globalization looks at how power divides between nations. Human-AI fit looks at how power divides between people and machines. It's the same question on two scales.</strong></p>
<p>Where you stand determines how AI treats you. So the last question is yours to answer:</p>
<p><strong>Where are you now? And where do you want to go?</strong></p>
<p>My advice is simple and concrete — don't think in the abstract, think one step forward:</p>
<p><strong>"In 2027, which kind of work will become *more* valuable because of AI? Go build there."</strong></p>
<p>Judgment, vertical understanding, and cross-domain connection only appreciate as AI gets stronger — because AI is responsible for doing things fast, and you're responsible for knowing what to do, why to do it, and who it serves.</p>
